📊 Student Score Analysis & Reporting

A Jupyter Notebook project that loads student score data from a CSV file, calculates key statistics, and visualizes the results with charts. Data can be loaded from a local path, a file upload, or a web link.

✨ Features
Three ways to load data
Enter a CSV file path
Upload a CSV file (via an interactive widget)
Load a CSV directly from a URL
Automatic calculations for every student:
Average score across all subjects
Max Score (best subject)
Result → Pass if average ≥ 50, otherwise Fail
Automatic subject detection: all numeric columns are treated as subjects, so the code works with different CSV files
Visualizations
Bar chart of each student's subject scores
Line graph of each student's average score
🗂️ Project Structure
.
├── Analysis_and_Reporting.ipynb   # Main notebook
└── README.md

Rename the notebook filename above to match your actual file.

🛠️ Requirements
Python 3.8+
Jupyter Notebook / JupyterLab (or VS Code with the Jupyter extension)
Libraries:
pandas
matplotlib
ipywidgets

Install them with:

bash
pip install pandas matplotlib ipywidgets notebook
🚀 How to Run
Clone the repository:
bash
   git clone https://github.com/kaleab158/Machine-Learning-journey/BreedMasterAI
   cd BreedMasterAI
Start Jupyter:
bash
   jupyter notebook
Open the notebook and run all cells.
When prompted, choose how to load your data:
Choice	Option	What to enter
1	CSV by path	Full or relative path, e.g. data/students.csv
2	Upload CSV	Use the upload button, then run the cell again
3	CSV from link	A direct URL to a .csv file
📄 Expected CSV Format
The first column should be the student name or ID (used as the chart label).
Text columns such as Gender are ignored in calculations.
All numeric columns are treated as subject scores.

Example:

Name	Gender	Maths	Physics	Chemistry	English	Biology	Economics	History	Civics
Student1	M	78	65	89	76	88	67	90	72
Student2	F	56	74	68	85	79	80	77	69
Student3	M	90	88	92	84	86	75	89	91
📈 Sample Output

After processing, three new columns are added:

Name	...	Average	Max Score	Result
Student1	...	78.125	90	Pass
Student7	...	39.500	45	Fail
Student50	...	96.500	100	Pass

Then two charts are displayed:

Student Subject Scores: grouped bar chart per student
Student Average Scores: line graph of averages with markers

(You can add screenshots here: ![Bar chart](images/bar_chart.png))

🧩 Code Overview
Function	Purpose
Data_Insertion(choice)	Loads the CSV based on the user's choice (path, upload, or URL)
Data_Calculation(df)	Computes Average, Max Score, and Pass/Fail result
Data_Visualization(df)	Draws the bar chart and line graph
⚠️ Notes
For the upload option, the file must be selected first, then the cell re-run to process it.
The notebook uses input() prompts, so run it in an interactive environment.
The pass mark is set to 50 and can be changed in Data_Calculation:
python
  df["Result"] = df["Average"].apply(lambda x: "Pass" if x >= 50 else "Fail")
🔮 Possible Improvements
Add ranking of students by average
Gender-based performance comparison
Subject-wise statistics (mean, highest, lowest)
Export the processed data to a new CSV or PDF report
Make the pass mark configurable
